Apple today added three Macs to its <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102772">list of obsolete products</a>. The 2018 Retina <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-air/">MacBook Air</a>, the 13-inch 2018 <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> with four Thunderbolt 3 ports, and the 13-inch 2017 &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; with two Thunderbolt 3 ports are now considered obsolete.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2018/10/MacBook-Air-family-10302018.jpg" alt="" width="2400" height="1056" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-663056" /><br/>
The three products were previously part of the vintage list, but have been moved. Apple adds a product to the vintage list when it's been five years since it was last distributed for sale, and it becomes obsolete at the seven-year mark.<br/>
<br/>
Vintage products can still be repaired at Apple retail locations and by Apple Authorized Service Providers if Apple has the parts available. Devices that are obsolete are generally not eligible for repair, and Apple stops making repair components.<br/>
<br/>
Apple has a battery-only repair policy for Mac laptops for up to 10 years after the Mac was last distributed for sale, so 2017/2018 &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj; and 2018 &zwnj;MacBook Air&zwnj; owners may still be able to get battery replacements.<br/>
<br/>
The 2018 &zwnj;MacBook Air&zwnj; was the first in the Air line with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/touch-id/">Touch ID</a> and a Retina display. Prior &zwnj;MacBook Air&zwnj; models had a non-Retina display with much lower resolution and less vibrant colors. 2018 was also a redesign year for the &zwnj;MacBook Air&zwnj;, with Apple introducing smaller bezels and a thinner, lighter chassis.<br/>
<br/>
